RK3588S Orange Pi 5B HDR10 output over HDMI.

Most of what HDR needs is already in 7.1.8. The HDMI connector helper builds
and sends the Dynamic Range and Mastering InfoFrame, dw-hdmi-qp implements the
packet writes behind it, and since patch 0003 this board runs the link at
10 bpc — 4K120 RGB over FRL6. What is missing is the other half of the
signalling, and the way it gets to the wire.

A sink stays in SDR unless the AVI InfoFrame also declares BT.2020, and the
colorimetry bits of that InfoFrame are filled from the "Colorspace" connector
property, which no bridge-based HDMI connector attaches. Userspace has nothing
to set, so HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA on its own changes nothing on screen and mutter
reports the output as not HDR-capable. Attach it in drm_bridge_connector.

Attach "Broadcast RGB" with it. On a CEA mode the helper defaults the
quantization range to limited while nothing in this path narrows the
framebuffer to match, so the sink is told to expand a signal that was never
compressed. Under PQ that is not a subtle shift: the code values are absolute
luminance, and userspace needs a way to say which range it is sending.

Delivery is the second half. Bridges write InfoFrames from atomic_enable, so a
commit that changes colorimetry or HDR metadata but no timing produces no
modeset and never reaches the sink — and turning HDR on is exactly such a
commit. Treat both as mode-changing in the HDMI atomic check, next to the bpc,
format and quantization-range changes already listed there.

One more state has to be explicit for this board: RGB range. The helper's
Automatic policy follows CTA and advertises limited range on CEA timings, while
Rockchip's scanout path is feeding full-range RGB buffers. After toggling HDR
back to SDR this ambiguous range state can leave applications such as Chromium
looking washed out until the display stack is restarted. Default to full-range
RGB, make the Automatic policy resolve to full-range on this path even if
userspace restores Automatic after login, and also treat changes in the derived
limited/full result as mode-changing so the AVI InfoFrame is always rewritten.

diff -Naur '--exclude=.git' '--exclude=*.orig' '--exclude=*.rej' '--exclude=include-prefixes' a/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_bridge_connector.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_bridge_connector.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_bridge_connector.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/display/drm_bridge_connector.c
@@ -989,6 +989,32 @@
 
 		connector->hdmi.max_frl_rate_per_lane =
 			bridge_connector->bridge_hdmi->max_frl_rate_per_lane;
+
+		/*
+		 * A sink leaves SDR only when it is told two things: BT.2020
+		 * in the colorimetry bits of the AVI InfoFrame, and PQ or HLG
+		 * in the HDR InfoFrame. The HDMI connector helper fills both
+		 * from connector state, but the colorimetry half is read from
+		 * a property that nothing attaches here, so the AVI InfoFrame
+		 * can otherwise only ever say "default".
+		 */
+		ret = drm_mode_create_hdmi_colorspace_property(connector, 0);
+		if (ret)
+			return ERR_PTR(ret);
+
+		drm_connector_attach_colorspace_property(connector);
+
+		/*
+		 * The CTA default asks for limited range on a CEA mode, and
+		 * nothing in this path narrows a full-range framebuffer to
+		 * match, so the sink is told to expand what was never
+		 * compressed. Attaching the property lets userspace say which
+		 * range it is actually sending; it matters more under PQ,
+		 * where the code values are absolute luminance.
+		 */
+		ret = drm_connector_attach_broadcast_rgb_property(connector);
+		if (ret)
+			return ERR_PTR(ret);
 	} else {
 		ret = drmm_connector_init(drm, connector,
 					  &drm_bridge_connector_funcs,
